### What are Vector API and security additions?

Recent JDK releases add performance and security APIs that were previously available only through third-party libraries.

### What is the Vector API?

The Vector API (incubating, JEP series) expresses SIMD (Single Instruction, Multiple Data) operations so a loop over numeric arrays compiles to hardware vector instructions. Instead of processing one value per instruction, the CPU processes 8, 16, or even 32 values simultaneously.

```java
package com.bank.risk;

import jdk.incubator.vector.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class RiskCalculator {

    // Process 8 doubles at once using AVX-512
    public double[] computeReturns(double[] prices, double[] prevPrices) {
        double[] returns = new double[prices.length];
        var species = DoubleVector.SPECIES_512;  // 8 doubles per vector

        for (int i = 0; i < prices.length; i += species.length()) {
            var price = DoubleVector.fromArray(species, prices, i);
            var prev = DoubleVector.fromArray(species, prevPrices, i);
            var ret = price.sub(prev).div(prev);  // (price - prev) / prev
            ret.intoArray(returns, i);
        }
        return returns;
    }
}
```

### What are the security APIs?

```java
package com.bank.security;

import java.security.*;
import javax.crypto.*;

public class SecurityService {

    // KDF: derive encryption key from master secret
    public SecretKey deriveKey(byte[] masterSecret, byte[] salt) throws Exception {
        KDF kdf = KDF.getInstance("HKDF-SHA256");
        return kdf.deriveKey("AES",
            KDF.HKDFParameterSpec.ofExtract().addIKM(masterSecret).addSalt(salt)
                .thenExpand(null, 32));
    }

    // PEM: read certificate from file
    public java.security.cert.X509Certificate loadCert(String pemFile) throws Exception {
        return java.security.PEM.decode(java.nio.file.Files.readString(
            java.nio.file.Path.of(pemFile)));
    }
}
```

### When should you use these APIs?

| API | Use when | Banking example |
|---|---|---|
| Vector API | Bulk numeric computation (risk, ML) | Portfolio risk across 10,000 positions |
| KDF API | Deriving keys from passwords/secrets | Per-session encryption keys |
| PEM encoding | Loading TLS certificates | mTLS between microservices |
| KEM API | Post-quantum key exchange | Forward-secure key agreement |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The Vector API is incubating (not final). Gate it behind benchmarks; it only helps when the loop is the bottleneck and the data fits in CPU cache.
- The security APIs standardize what previously required Bouncy Castle or other third-party crypto libraries.
- In regulated banking environments, using JDK-native crypto APIs simplifies compliance audits.
- The KEM API with Kyber is important for NIST post-quantum migration timelines.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Does the Vector API help all numeric code? (No. Only tight loops over large arrays that the JIT cannot auto-vectorize effectively.)
- What is HKDF? (HMAC-based Key Derivation Function: extracts and expands keying material from a shared secret.)
- Why is PEM encoding important? (PEM is the standard text format for certificates in TLS, SSH, and signing. The API eliminates hand-parsing.)
